+
+NOTES = r"""
+Definition and construction.  Definition 1 and Theorem 3 state the generalized
+Bose--Chowla construction in cyclic-exponent notation.  Section 3 proves it in
+the native field objects actually used here: if beta has degree h, equality of
+two h-fold products of beta+v values makes two monic degree-h polynomials agree
+at beta; their leading terms cancel, so degree(beta)=h forces the polynomials,
+and hence their factor multisets, to be equal.  We use h=3 over F_p.
+
+Step-0 algorithm check.  The proof itself shows that decoding a target is a
+split-cubic factorization problem.  Cantor--Zassenhaus is a randomized expected
+polynomial-time algorithm in degree and log(p), so Track A would be false.  This
+module declares Track B and includes a general equal-degree factorizer as its
+successful reference algorithm.  The planted cubics have a shorter route: their
+three constants form a reflected triple c-d,c,c+d.  For
+P(x)=(x+c)^3-d^2(x+c), centering gives c from the quadratic coefficient and
+d^2 from the linear coefficient.  The generator never runs either route: it
+samples c,d first and composes the target product.
+
+Easy regimes and attacks.  Tiny p is hand-enumerable, and any disclosed factor
+is removable by polynomial division, so the task uses large p and discloses no
+factor.  Plants use a uniformly random center and a random nonzero bounded step.
+The attack panel tests raw-coefficient outliers, the first three residues, 256
+structure-aware random triples, and the tempting but incomplete adjacent-factor
+ansatz d=1.  General Cantor--Zassenhaus is reported separately, as Track B
+requires.  The untested alternatives are specialized cubic formulas and CAS
+factorization; both are expected to succeed and are noted in README.md.
+""".strip()
+
