     1	# arXiv 2308.12406 — affine equivalence of Bose `B_3`-sets
     2	
     3	**Status: locally verified, awaiting oracle evidence.** Every executable gate passes,
     4	but the mandatory OpenRouter run produced only HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” service
     5	errors. The family is therefore neither hardened nor rejected and must not be emitted
     6	until the three oracle arms are rerun with a funded key.
     7	
     8	| profile field | value |
     9	|---|---|
    10	| Track | B — an efficient exact algorithm is acknowledged |
    11	| native domain | number theory |
    12	| object regime | finite discrete |
    13	| computational core | other (modular affine equivalence) |
    14	| certificate | integer tuple `[d,s]` |
    15	| intuition | invariant: centered power sums scale homogeneously |
    16	| domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
    17	
    18	## Problem and provenance
    19	
    20	The source is Kevin O’Bryant, [*Constructing Thick `B_h`-sets*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12406).
    21	Definition 1 and Section 3 construct a generalized Bose set in
    22	`Z/(q^h-1)Z`. Theorem 3 proves it is a `B_h`-set: an `h`-sum collision becomes
    23	equality of two split degree-`h` polynomials over `F_q`. The definition immediately
    24	after that theorem makes affine equivalence of these modular sets explicit, and
    25	Theorem 4 studies parameter choices that produce equivalent sets.
    26	
    27	Here `h=3`. The generator builds a Bose set using a primitive cubic, samples a
    28	subset (which remains `B_3`), samples `[d,s]` first, and forms an independently
    29	shuffled image `B=dA+s`. A solver receives both native modular `B_3`-sets and must
    30	give any promised affine equivalence. `verify` independently enumerates all
    31	three-term multisets to confirm both inputs are `B_3`, checks that `d` is a unit,
    32	and compares the exact modular image with `B`.
    33	
    34	## Why Track B
    35	
    36	The paper supplies constructions and sufficient affine-equivalence conditions; it
    37	does not prove hardness for this inverse-planted distribution. Section 2 describes
    38	finite-field computation and “computerized labor,” Section 6 exhaustively examines
    39	small affine images/subsets, and Section 7 asks for faster interpretations of some
    40	Theorem 4 conditions. Those facts cannot support Track A.
    41	
    42	The acknowledged reference algorithm chooses one invertible ordered pair in `A`,
    43	tries every ordered pair in `B`, derives `[d,s]`, and compares images. It is
    44	`O(k^3)`. At shipping `q=47,k=15`, the latest audit solved 8/8 instances in 48,034
    45	counted exact operations and 0.043 seconds total (about 6,004 operations each).
