+NOTES = r"""
+Definition and construction.  Definition 1 and Theorem 3 state the generalized
+Bose--Chowla construction in cyclic-exponent notation.  Section 3 proves it in
+the native field objects actually used here: if beta has degree h, equality of
+two h-fold products of beta+v values makes two monic degree-h polynomials agree
+at beta; their leading terms cancel, so degree(beta)=h forces the polynomials,
+and hence their factor multisets, to be equal.  We use h=11 over F_p.
+
+Step-0 algorithm check.  The proof itself shows that decoding a target is a
+split-polynomial factorization problem.  Cantor--Zassenhaus is a randomized expected
+polynomial-time algorithm in degree and log(p), so Track A would be false.  This
+module declares Track B and includes a general equal-degree factorizer as its
+successful reference algorithm.  The planted polynomials have a shorter route:
+their eleven constants form the reflected arithmetic progression
+c-5d,...,c+5d.  The two coefficients below the leading term give c and d^2
+directly.  The generator never runs either route: it samples c,d first and
+composes the target product.
+
+Easy regimes and attacks.  Tiny p is approachable by direct root trials, and any
+disclosed factor is removable by polynomial division, so the task uses large p
+and discloses no factor.  Plants use a uniformly random center and a random
+nonzero bounded step.  The attack panel tests raw-coefficient outliers, the
+first eleven residues, 256 structure-aware random factor multisets, and the
+tempting but incomplete adjacent-factor
+ansatz d=1.  General Cantor--Zassenhaus is reported separately, as Track B
+requires.  The untested alternatives are other finite-field factorization algorithms and CAS
+factorization; both are expected to succeed and are noted in README.md.
+""".strip()
+
